## Security Checklist for Secret Management

### Before Adding Any Secret:
- [ ] Add secret only to `.env` (development) or secret management vault (production)
- [ ] Commit `.env.example` with placeholder values (NOT real secrets)
- [ ] Add appropriate secret names to `.gitignore`
- [ ] Document the secret in code comments or documentation
- [ ] Set up secret access controls with your cloud provider

### Before Going to Production:
- [ ] All secrets loaded from environment (NOT hardcoded)
- [ ] Require secret rotation policy (change every 90 days)
- [ ] Set up secret access monitoring
- [ ] Configure secret expiration for temporary access
- [ ] Regular security audits of secret usage

---

## Threat Model for Secret Management

| Threat | Risk Level | Mitigation |
|--------|------------|------------|
| `.env` committed to git | **HIGH** | `.gitignore` + PR review + GitHub commit security checks |
| Hardcoded secrets in code | **CRITICAL** | Never commit code with secrets, maybe use remote code signing |
| Exposed secrets in logs | **HIGH** | Strip secrets from all log output, use log masking |
| Compromised CI/CD account | **CRITICAL** | Restrict CI account permissions, use separate secrets for tests |
| Database credentials stolen | **CRITICAL** | Database accounts with least privilege, read-only for apps |
| Access key compromised | **HIGH** | IAM roles with least privilege, rotate keys immediately |
| Secret rotation failure | **MEDIUM-HIGH** | Automated secret rotation process, redundancy, backup policies |

---

